.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMO-UPDATE-ALERT-RULE 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emo-update-alert-rule \- change a threshold alert rule
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli emo update-alert-rule
.B \-\-rule\-id
.I ID
[any
.BR euclid-cli-emo-create-alert-rule (1)
option]
[\fB\-\-no\-warning\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-critical\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-labels\fR]
[\fB\-\-enable\fR | \fB\-\-disable\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Changes only what is given; everything else stays as it was. The result
is checked the way a new rule is, so a change that would leave the rule
without a line, or with its lines the wrong way round, is refused and
nothing is changed.
.SH WHAT A CHANGE DOES TO ITS ALERTS
.TP
A changed line, statistic or period count
Applies from the next period, to the series as they stand. A CRITICAL
series judged against a raised critical line clears once it has been
inside for the rule's clear periods \- the change somebody making it
wants to see.
.TP
A changed metric or label selector
The rule now judges other series, so it ends what it said about the old
ones: each one not OK is cleared and published as
.I emo.alert.cleared
with reason
.IR rule-changed ,
and the new series start from OK. Otherwise a subscriber that was told
CRITICAL would wait for a clear the rule can no longer send.
.TP
.B \-\-disable
The same, with reason
.IR rule-disabled .
The rule is kept and nothing is judged until
.BR \-\-enable .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rule\-id " " \fIID\fR
The rule to change. Required; a rule's id cannot itself be changed.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-label " " \fINAME\fR=\fIVALUE\fR
Replaces the whole selector with the labels given, rather than adding to
it.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-warning
Removes the warning line.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-critical
Removes the critical line. A rule needs at least one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-labels
Judges every series of the metric.
.TP
.B \-\-enable
Evaluates the rule from the next period.
.TP
.B \-\-disable
Stops evaluating it, clearing what it raised.
.PP
The remaining options are those of
.BR euclid-cli-emo-create-alert-rule (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The rule was changed and is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
The caller was not authenticated or is not an administrator; there is no
rule of that id (HTTP 404); or the changed rule is not valid (HTTP 400).
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# Raise the warning line
euclid-cli emo update-alert-rule --rule-id system-cpu-per-host --warning 85

# Wait ten minutes before alerting
euclid-cli emo update-alert-rule --rule-id system-cpu-per-host --for-periods 2

# Only one host from now on
euclid-cli emo update-alert-rule --rule-id system-cpu-per-host --label host=vogje01-nuc

# Off for a maintenance window, and on again
euclid-cli emo update-alert-rule --rule-id system-cpu-per-host --disable
euclid-cli emo update-alert-rule --rule-id system-cpu-per-host --enable
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emo (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emo-create-alert-rule (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emo-delete-alert-rule (1)
